#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "47507b72-86e2-5980-9e59-22d59c9f7279" :question/math-academy-id "e-9918" :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} & =(12.5)^{2}+(11.7)^{2}+(13.2)^{2}+(12.8)^{2}+(12.1)^{2} \\\\  & =156.25+136.89+174.24+163.84+146.41 \\\\  & =777.63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "11f8e3f1-5110-5db1-83de-99d65ea76de5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9920" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left( \\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i.$ \n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3711c26b-4707-57d9-bc3a-ae24345bf877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.24\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416872} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3fbe09-85be-535f-ad1f-23b1ff44f89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416876} {:answer/id #uuid "8554a30d-6e9e-5d98-ae53-faadfb7e4fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.34\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416887} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfdabea-00d1-569e-bbdd-3b4970985102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.28\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416890} {:answer/id #uuid "b72f54bc-2f78-5a2a-a357-15c7b1e0a9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.01\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e3fbe09-85be-535f-ad1f-23b1ff44f89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.11\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416876} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be518d5-8e65-51e4-8c16-7752d6693d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4505d831-af23-5580-85dc-c9dcc976f63a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139782" :question/problem "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n1.34,\\,1.24,\\,1.42,\\,1.28,\\,1.39,\\,1.44\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}$ for this particular sample." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=1.34+1.24+1.42+1.28+1.39+1.44=8.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $8.11\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416864} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416869} {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416902} {:answer/id #uuid "e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} {:answer/id #uuid "7e200172-34ca-5ab5-a0aa-e59cf6e4d94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416884} {:answer/id #uuid "8162f83a-7db2-5f3b-82a9-fd24f5cd3429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416886} {:answer/id #uuid "9432a7e4-d864-5909-96fb-bc5b42aaf049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416891} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f74fae-3121-59b5-8358-f435d8ddb989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416874} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139820" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n- $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5851829e-e164-50e4-a521-b2e5029611e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416879} {:answer/id #uuid "61f7e4c9-a328-59a5-8c5e-a513b1c8edcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.3$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416881} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca42f7b-82c6-5808-b73e-445211c3621a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416893} {:answer/id #uuid "ab40a5fb-1670-5156-9f84-cc7d989d494a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416896} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b8c0-a1a1-5ebd-91a9-014ae312419a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5851829e-e164-50e4-a521-b2e5029611e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416879} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60edaef-c13b-5348-91f8-4e69a22c173f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "264130f3-38ba-510d-80d5-15a003677fae" :question/math-academy-id "q-149837" :question/problem "The ages, in years, of six randomly selected college students drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$$\n17,\\,19,\\,21,\\,18,\\,20,\\,19\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the following statistic for this particular sample.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n})+4}{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- ${X}_{i}$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n\n- ${x}_{i}$ is the value of ${X}_{i}$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{min}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})=17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $\\frac{17+4}{6}=3.5$ years."}]}
